            },
            {
              "type": "heading-one",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Cubans in Puerto Rico: Migration, Associational Life, and Community Memory",
                  "italic": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "The history of Cuban migration to Puerto Rico cannot be understood exclusively through the political transformations of 1959 or the experiences of those who subsequently identified themselves as exiles. Movement between the two islands belongs to a longer Caribbean history of political encounters, cultural exchanges, and personal relationships. Yet the Cuban Revolution and the migrations that followed transformed the meanings of departure, displacement, and belonging, generating different ways of narrating the relationship between Cuba and Puerto Rico."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "For Cubans who established their lives in Puerto Rico, migration involved more than the geographical relocation of individuals and families. It also entailed the formation of social networks, cultural associations, economic activities, and institutions through which connections to Cuba could be maintained, reinterpreted, or contested. These spaces participated in the production of community memory, although the experiences and political perspectives represented within them were neither uniform nor necessarily shared by subsequent generations."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "grid",
      "layout": [
        4,
        2
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "",
                  "bold": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "bold": true,
                  "text": "Cubans in Puerto Rico: Migration, Associational Life, and Community Memory"
                },
                {
                  "text": " examines how these histories have been documented, represented, and preserved across audiovisual recordings, newspapers, institutional publications, photographs, and other cultural materials. Bringing Natalia Bonilla’s documentary "
                },
                {
                  "text": "Ecos del Exilio",
                  "italic": true
                },
                {
                  "text": " (2013) into conversation with historical and contemporary sources, the project investigates the relationships between personal testimony, institutional narratives, public memory, and the changing meanings of Cuban belonging in Puerto Rico."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "The project also approaches the archive as a site of selection and mediation. Different sources preserve different accounts of migration and community life, raising questions about whose experiences become visible, whose perspectives acquire institutional authority, and what remains difficult to recover from the surviving record."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Through audiovisual annotation and contextual research, this digital project invites readers to examine these materials comparatively rather than encounter them as a unified narrative of Cuban migration. Its broader purpose is to investigate how community formation and historical memory emerge through relationships among lived experience, cultural practices, institutions, and the records through which those relationships become known."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
